    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d147-a1">

                <pb facs="0240" n="132" sameAs="frus:frus1893/pg132"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No. 173.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Naval commandants to diplomatic corps.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">U.
                                S. Cruiser Charleston</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1894-01-11"><hi rendition="#italic">January 11,
                        1894</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>The senior naval representations of the United States of America, France,
                    Portugal, England, and Italy have the honor to inform their respective
                    diplomatic representatives that they have observed with great surprise the
                    firing from heavy guns from the battery on Coquerida (Bom-Jesus) Island at two
                    vessels of the insurgents, the <hi rendition="#italic">Trajano</hi> and <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Guandbara</hi>, lying to the northward and westward a
                    short distance from Euxadas Island. This firing was commenced by the guns on
                    Coquerida Island, and was replied to by the heavy guns on the two vessels. The
                    zone fired over was that in which the merchant ships of many nations lie at
                    anchor; a premature explosion of shells from either side, which not infrequently
                    happens, might have destroyed both life and property.</p>
                <p>This seems a strange coincidence after the letter received by the senior
                    commanders, through their diplomatic representatives, from the ministry of
                    foreign affairs for the Brazilian Government, dated January 8, and also after
                    the fact that this position for anchoring was given us verbally but officially
                    by the captain of the port, and we inclose a communication which shows this to
                    be the case absolutely.</p>
                <closer><signed>
                        <list>
                            <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">A. Aubry</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                    rendition="#italic">Commanding Naval Forces of
                                Italy.</hi></item>
                            <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">S. Rolleston</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                    rendition="#italic">Commanding English Naval Forces</hi>.</item>
                            <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">Augusto de Costilho</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                    rendition="#italic">Commanding Naval Forces of
                                Portugal</hi>.</item>
                            <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">De Barbeyrac</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                    rendition="#italic">Commanding the Naval Forces of
                                France</hi>.</item>
                            <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">Henry F. Picking</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                    rendition="#italic">Commanding Naval Forces of the United States
                                    of America.</hi></item>
                        </list>
                    </signed></closer>
            </div>
